user_prompt:
Brand: Dior (category: Fashion, Apparel & Footwear)
Topic from the client: repair and reuse

You are continuing an idea development worksheet for one campaign for this brand on this topic. Four candidate campaign directions have already been written:

[
  {
    "id": "C1",
    "title": "Dior Lineage",
    "core_idea": "Every significant Dior piece is given a lineage record covering its atelier, first owner and repairs. When a piece is passed on or resold through a Dior-verified channel, the atelier refreshes it and the new owner is formally welcomed, with the previous owner's note attached. Reuse becomes inheritance and provenance, not second-hand.",
    "brand_connection": "Builds on Dior's heritage, archive culture and the authority of its ateliers to authenticate and restore.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Makes resale feel prestigious and keeps value and control in the Dior ecosystem",
      "Fights counterfeits through provenance",
      "Emotional handover stories with strong content potential"
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Resale could cannibalise new sales or be seen as devaluing the brand",
      "Heavy operational and data infrastructure",
      "More of a business model than a culturally sparking idea"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C2",
    "title": "The New Look, Again",
    "core_idea": "Each season, Dior's ateliers invite clients to bring in a treasured piece from any year. It could be a tired Bar jacket, a worn Lady Dior or an inherited coat. The ateliers re-cut, repair and reinterpret it into the season's silhouette. A selection of these transformed pieces appears in a runway moment and a film series alongside the new collection. Reuse becomes a creative act, echoing how the 1947 New Look reinvented fashion by reshaping what already existed.",
    "brand_connection": "The New Look was a radical act of reinvention. Dior's craft authority lives in the ateliers, and its archive and long-lived icons (Bar, Lady Dior) are built to outlast trends. The idea makes 'Dior is always becoming' a lived service.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Turns repair from a defensive service into a creative, desirable event that earns fashion-press and social attention",
      "Repeatable seasonal platform with a built-in runway, film, editorial and client-event structure",
      "Strong emotional storytelling, because every piece comes with a real owner and a memory",
      "Reinforces long-term ownership and the lifetime value of icons without discounting"
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Atelier capacity limits scale, so access could feel elitist",
      "Could be read as a one-off stunt unless it becomes a standing service",
      "Needs credible, specific impact proof to avoid greenwashing accusations"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C3",
    "title": "The Mended Star",
    "core_idea": "Dior introduces a signature visible-mending mark: a tiny hand-embroidered star, drawn from Christian Dior's lucky charm, stitched over repairs. Repaired pieces become badges of love and longevity, and the mark becomes a status code, a sign that a piece has been cherished enough to be fixed.",
    "brand_connection": "Draws on Dior's star superstition, craft embroidery and the idea of the talisman. It gives the brand an ownable visual code for repair.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "A simple, highly shareable visual symbol that can spread through street style and social media",
      "Reframes flaws as luck and love, which is emotionally warm and brand-specific",
      "Easy to apply across product categories"
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Risks sparking fake distress and 'damage as fashion', encouraging people to break pieces for the mark",
      "Some luxury clients may not want visible evidence of repair on a pristine piece",
      "More of a graphic device than a full campaign platform"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C4",
    "title": "Petites Mains Open Hours",
    "core_idea": "Dior's artisans set up pop-up repair clinics in cities around the world and fix any garment from any brand, including fast fashion, for free. Passers-by watch couture hands mend a high-street hoodie. Dior positions skill, not logo, as the ultimate luxury and publicly shares repair craft.",
    "brand_connection": "Puts the petites mains, the heart of Dior's authority, in front of the public and shows the brand's generosity of skill.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Provocative and highly newsworthy, with a strong human-interest angle",
      "Reaches non-clients and builds goodwill and cultural relevance",
      "Elevates artisans as the true stars of the brand"
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Could dilute exclusivity if skill is perceived as commoditised",
      "Costly and capacity-hungry, and risks looking like a charity stunt",
      "Weak link to Dior's own products and ownership, so unclear commercial return"
    ]
  }
]

The selection section is already complete:

{
  "selection": {
    "selected_id": "C2",
    "reasons_for_selection": [
      "It is the only direction that makes repair and reuse a creative act rather than a service or a business model. That fits a house whose founding story, the 1947 New Look, was about reshaping what already existed. The idea is anchored in Dior's own history, not borrowed from a sustainability playbook.",
      "It sits where Dior's authority is strongest: the ateliers, the archive and the long-lived icons (Bar jacket, Lady Dior). It does not depend on Dior inventing new credibility. It shows craft authority in action.",
      "Repair becomes desirable, not defensive. A runway moment, a film series and editorial content give it the cultural spark and press interest that a quiet aftercare service would not earn. It speaks to fashion audiences in the language they already care about: silhouette, reinvention and craft.",
      "It is platformable. The seasonal rhythm gives a repeatable structure (call for pieces, atelier transformation, runway, film, client events) that can run for years and keep generating fresh stories. Each season's cohort brings its own owners, memories and garments.",
      "It reinforces ownership and lifetime value without discounting or pushing second-hand. Clients are encouraged to keep and reinvent pieces, which supports the brand's pricing power and avoids the cannibalisation risk that comes with a resale model.",
      "Its main concerns can be managed. Capacity limits can be handled by treating it as a curated seasonal event alongside a permanent, scalable atelier repair and alteration service. That also answers the 'one-off stunt' risk. Specific, verifiable impact proof (pieces kept in use, hours of atelier work, materials reused) can be published each season to guard against greenwashing. Scarcity is also consistent with luxury, provided selection is transparent and open to clients across regions and eras."
    ],
    "rejected": [
      {
        "id": "C1",
        "reason": "Dior Lineage is commercially smart and strong on provenance and anti-counterfeit, but it is more an operating model than a campaign idea. It needs heavy data and logistics infrastructure before it can say anything culturally. It also invites the resale-cannibalisation and brand-devaluation debate, which puts Dior on the defensive. It works better as a later enabling layer, for example giving transformed C2 pieces a lineage record, than as the lead idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C3",
        "reason": "The Mended Star is an appealing, ownable symbol with warm roots in Dior's lucky star, but it is a graphic device more than a platform. It risks encouraging deliberate damage for the mark, which would undercut the longevity message. Many luxury clients also do not want visible repair on a pristine piece. It could be a small signature detail inside C2, but not the central idea."
      },
      {
        "id": "C4",
        "reason": "Petites Mains Open Hours is provocative and generous and would earn goodwill, but it is the weakest link to Dior's own products and ownership, so the commercial return is unclear. It is costly and capacity-hungry, and it risks looking like a charity stunt or commoditising skill, which could dilute the exclusivity that supports the brand. C2 keeps the spotlight on the artisans while staying tied to Dior's pieces and clients."
      }
    ]
  }
}

Complete the remaining sections of the worksheet:

1. revisions_after_selection: changes made to the recommended idea after reviewing it, with the reason for each. Use an empty list if there are none.
2. final_proposal: the recommended idea written for a human reader (at most 150 words), plus exactly 3 open questions that would help the reader develop further ideas of their own.

Write in English. Return these sections as a single JSON object with this structure and nothing else:
{
  "revisions_after_selection": [{"change": "...", "reason": "..."}],
  "final_proposal": {"title": "...", "idea": "...", "follow_up_questions": ["...", "...", "..."]}
}

text:
{
  "revisions_after_selection": [
    {
      "change": "Paired the seasonal event with a permanent atelier repair and alteration service, so the runway moment is the visible peak of an always-on offer.",
      "reason": "This answers the 'one-off stunt' concern and the capacity limits. Clients who are not selected still have a route to keep and renew their pieces."
    },
    {
      "change": "Made selection transparent and open to clients across regions, eras and piece types, with published criteria. Pieces are chosen for story and transformability, not for value or status.",
      "reason": "This reduces the risk that access feels elitist, and it keeps the stories varied and emotionally rich."
    },
    {
      "change": "Added a seasonal impact ledger with specific, verifiable figures: pieces kept in use, atelier hours, materials reused and pieces repaired through the standing service.",
      "reason": "Specific proof guards against greenwashing accusations and gives the idea credibility beyond the spectacle."
    },
    {
      "change": "Positioned the Mended Star (C3) and the Lineage record (C1) as optional later layers, not core parts of the idea. The star would be a discreet, owner-chosen signature, and each transformed piece would receive a lineage record.",
      "reason": "This keeps the platform focused on reinvention. It also takes the useful parts of the rejected directions without bringing in the damage-for-the-mark and resale-cannibalisation risks."
    }
  ],
  "final_proposal": {
    "title": "The New Look, Again",
    "idea": "In 1947, Christian Dior reshaped fashion by reimagining what already existed. Each season, Dior's ateliers invite clients to bring a treasured piece from any year: a tired Bar jacket, a worn Lady Dior, an inherited coat. The petites mains re-cut, repair and reinterpret it into the season's silhouette. A selection appears in a runway moment and a film series beside the new collection, each with its owner and memory. Selection criteria are published and open to clients worldwide. A permanent atelier repair and alteration service keeps the idea running year-round, and a seasonal ledger shows pieces kept in use, atelier hours and materials reused. Reuse becomes a creative act, and Dior shows it is always becoming.",
    "follow_up_questions": [
      "How could the selection process itself become part of the story, so that being chosen, and not being chosen, still deepens a client's bond with the house?",
      "What would the standing, everyday version of this idea look like for a client whose piece never reaches the runway, and how could it feel as special as the seasonal event?",
      "How might the creative director's own design language meet the owner's memory, so that each transformation is a true collaboration and not just a restoration?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "writing_notes": {
    "intended_reader": "The reader is a Dior marketing or brand lead, or a client-side decision maker, who knows the house, its couture heritage and the luxury category well. They know the brief was 'repair and reuse' and have seen four directions. They are not couture tailors, so they may not know what a seam allowance is or how it works.",
    "reader_needs": [
      {
        "point": "What the idea is: a hidden inch of fabric inside Dior garments becomes the house's visible promise of a long life.",
        "why": "If the reader cannot repeat the idea in one breath, they cannot sell it inside the company."
      },
      {
        "point": "Why it is credible: it starts from a real construction detail, not a claim.",
        "why": "Luxury sustainability messages are often doubted, so a verifiable product truth is the main protection against greenwashing accusations."
      },
      {
        "point": "What people can actually do: book a refit, let out or take in a piece, and pass it on.",
        "why": "The reader needs to see that this is a service and behaviour, not only a message."
      },
      {
        "point": "What must be true for it to work: a published construction standard, atelier capacity, and a way to extend the idea to leather and shoes.",
        "why": "The reader needs to know the commitments and risks before approving anything."
      }
    ],
    "terms_to_explain": [
      {
        "term": "Seam allowance",
        "explanation": "The extra strip of fabric left inside a seam, beyond the stitch line, which can be used to resize the garment later."
      },
      {
        "term": "Refit",
        "explanation": "An atelier appointment that adjusts an owned garment to the wearer's current body, by taking it in or letting it out."
      },
      {
        "term": "Construction standard",
        "explanation": "A published set of rules for how much allowance and what build quality Dior pieces must have, so the promise can be checked."
      }
    ],
    "tone_choices": [
      {
        "choice": "Write in plain, confident sentences with no sustainability jargon.",
        "reason": "The idea works because it avoids a lecture, and the writing should show the same restraint."
      },
      {
        "choice": "Lead with the image of the hidden inch before explaining the mechanics.",
        "reason": "A concrete, visual picture makes the technical detail feel emotional and memorable rather than dry."
      },
      {
        "choice": "State the conditions for credibility openly, in one short line.",
        "reason": "Naming the commitment needed builds trust with a reader who will worry about greenwashing."
      }
    ],
    "outline": [
      "Name the idea and the hidden inch in one image.",
      "Explain what a seam allowance is and why it is a true Dior product detail.",
      "Show the shift in meaning: the garment is built to change with its owner.",
      "List what people can do: refit, let out or take in, and pass on.",
      "Show how the inch becomes a platform: a visual device, a service name and a design standard, stretched to leather and shoes as 'the next inch'.",
      "State what Dior must commit to so the promise holds."
    ],
    "clarity_checks": [
      {
        "check": "Every technical term is explained at first use.",
        "how": "I will scan for 'seam allowance', 'refit' and 'standard' and make sure each comes with a plain explanation in the same sentence."
      },
      {
        "check": "The proposal stays within 150 words.",
        "how": "I will count the words in the idea text and cut any sentence that does not serve the reader's four needs."
      },
      {
        "check": "The idea can be summarised in one sentence.",
        "how": "I will test that the first two sentences alone state the idea and its proof."
      },
      {
        "check": "Claims are specific and not inflated.",
        "how": "I will replace vague words such as 'sustainable' or 'forever' with concrete actions, such as 'refit' and 'pass on'."
      }
    ]
  },
  "revisions_after_selection": [
    {
      "change": "Added a published construction standard, with minimum seam allowances for new pieces, as a visible part of the idea.",
      "reason": "This answers the greenwashing concern directly by making the promise checkable and turning a hidden detail into a commitment."
    },
    {
      "change": "Gave the stretch to leather goods and shoes a defined form: resoling, re-edging and re-strapping, all framed as 'the next inch'.",
      "reason": "Seam allowances do not apply to non-textile products, so the metaphor needed a clear extension."
    },
    {
      "change": "Borrowed real owners' stories from C1 and atelier hands from C4 as the emotional layer.",
      "reason": "This offsets the risk that the technical detail feels dry, and uses the strongest parts of the rejected ideas."
    },
    {
      "change": "Positioned 'owner number two' resale from C3 as a later extension, not part of the launch.",
      "reason": "It keeps the launch focused and avoids the internal business-model alignment that resale would require."
    }
  ],
  "final_proposal": {
    "title": "The Extra Inch",
    "idea": "Inside every well-made Dior garment is a hidden inch: spare fabric in the seams, called a seam allowance, that lets the piece be taken in or let out over decades. The campaign makes this inch the house's quiet promise that a Dior piece is built to change with its owner. People can book a refit, adjust a favourite piece, or pass it on, each framed as 'the next inch' of its story. For leather and shoes, resoling, re-edging and re-strapping play the same role. Real owners and atelier hands tell the stories. To keep the promise honest, Dior publishes a construction standard and commits atelier capacity for refits.",
    "follow_up_questions": [
      "What minimum inch, and what refit capacity, could Dior commit to publicly without overpromising?",
      "How could the inch become something owners can see or hold, such as a label, a ruler or a ritual at the refit?",
      "When a piece moves to a second owner, what should the next inch look like, and who should be allowed to tell that story?"
    ]
  }
}

