        "source": "C2",
        "purpose": "Would add a generosity gesture and a photogenic symbol.",
        "include": false,
        "reason": "It is familiar, risks pity and awkwardness, and dilutes the stance that the lane is for everyone. The physical lane sign is already a strong visual."
      },
      {
        "element": "A measured, opt-in pilot with off-peak or flexible-hours timing and published results.",
        "source": "new",
        "purpose": "Tests the merchant throughput concern, builds a business case, and separates a movement from a stunt.",
        "include": true,
        "reason": "The selection reasoning relies on this to manage the main risks. It makes the idea credible to retailers and gives the campaign a sustained commitment rather than a one-off activation."
      },
      {
        "element": "Open invitation framing: 'Slow Lane for anyone', with shoppers choosing the pace and no signal that the lane is for lonely people.",
        "source": "new",
        "purpose": "Protects against stigma and keeps the lane attractive to people who are simply not in a hurry.",
        "include": true,
        "reason": "This is the idea's main advantage over C1 and C2. Stating it explicitly in the design and comms stops the lane from being read as a lane for the lonely."
      },
      {
        "element": "Partnership with a loneliness or older-people charity to advise on the lane and share its knowledge.",
        "source": "new",
        "purpose": "Gives credibility on the loneliness issue, helps train cashiers on conversation without clinical framing, and guards against the idea feeling like a brand stunt.",
