Brand: Canva (category: B2B, SaaS & Productivity)
Topic from the client: the first paycheck

You are continuing an idea development worksheet for one campaign for this brand on this topic. Four candidate campaign directions have already been written:

[
  {
    "id": "C1",
    "title": "First Invoice",
    "core_idea": "For freelancers, creators and side-hustlers, the first 'paycheck' is the first invoice they send. Canva reframes it as the moment you become a business, with invoice, rate-card and brand-kit templates and the message 'Get paid like a pro'.",
    "brand_connection": "Links to Canva's small-business and Teams offer and its brand kit and document tools, with real productivity value.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Clear commercial benefit and a direct link to paid plans.",
      "Reaches the growing freelance and creator economy.",
      "Practical and credible."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "It narrows the topic to the self-employed and loses the broader emotional universality of a first paycheck.",
      "Invoice templates are a crowded utility space, so the idea may feel functional rather than culturally resonant.",
      "It is less likely to spark organic sharing."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C2",
    "title": "Frame It",
    "core_idea": "Treat the first payslip or cheque as a keepsake. Canva offers templates that let people redact and remix it into a framed piece of art, a scrapbook page or a story post, honouring a moment that normally ends up in a drawer.",
    "brand_connection": "A direct use of Canva's core strength: turning ordinary documents into beautiful, shareable design with almost no skill.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Easy to understand and highly shareable, since it fits existing social behaviour.",
      "Strong template-led product demonstration.",
      "Emotional and warm."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "It is nostalgic and personal but weak on B2B and productivity relevance.",
      "It carries privacy risks, because payslips contain sensitive data.",
      "It is a seasonal, one-time use case with limited platform life."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C3",
    "title": "Decode the Deductions",
    "core_idea": "Payslips baffle first-time earners, with gross and net, tax codes and contributions. Canva partners with payroll providers or educators to redesign the payslip into a clear visual explainer: where your money went and why.",
    "brand_connection": "Canva as the champion of clear visual communication, applying the belief that good design makes complex things understandable.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Real insight: the confusion and 'where did it go?' shock are universal.",
      "Useful and PR-friendly.",
      "Opens the door to partnerships with payroll and edtech brands."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "It moves into financial education, a category where Canva has little authority and some legal and accuracy risk.",
      "It is regionally specific because tax systems differ.",
      "It risks a lecturing tone and a less ownable role for Canva's product."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "C4",
    "title": "Paid For This",
    "core_idea": "Show the first paycheck next to the thing it paid for. Every first pay is a receipt for a piece of work: a menu, a pitch deck, a flyer, a logo, a slide, a social calendar. The campaign flips the usual photo of the payslip and celebrates the work itself with the line 'Paid for this', with the amount hidden by default. People pair their first piece of paid work with a feeling or a story instead of a figure.",
    "brand_connection": "Canva is the place where much of that first work gets made. The campaign positions Canva as the tool behind work people get paid for and shows that its output is professional-grade, while the 'work' lens fits its B2B, SaaS and productivity category.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Fresh, human and ownable angle: the paycheck becomes proof of ability, not of salary.",
      "Highly platformable as a UGC format, a template, OOH featuring real first work, a creator series and employer onboarding.",
      "Naturally inclusive of interns, gig workers, freelancers and small-business owners.",
      "Reveals the quality of work made in Canva without feeling like a product demo."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Not every first job involved making something visual or in Canva.",
      "Participants may want to share amounts, which could invite comparison or pay-gap debate.",
      "It needs strong seeding to avoid feeling like a generic hashtag trend."
    ]
  }
]

