    "core_idea": "Reframe wear as value. Dior invites owners to keep the scuffs, creases and patina that show a life lived, and repairs only what is needed, with a visible, signed repair gesture such as a hand-stitched Dior mend, a gold thread or a small atelier mark. Repair becomes a 'second signature' that makes a piece more desirable, not less. Campaign imagery celebrates imperfection with the same reverence as a runway look.",
    "brand_connection": "Draws on the atelier's mastery and the idea that couture is made by human hands, so a human-made mend is the logical next chapter of the garment. It also echoes the Japanese kintsugi sensibility without copying it, keeping Dior's own French vocabulary of thread, needle and hand.",
    "expected_strengths": [
      "Turns a perceived flaw into status, which is a genuinely surprising luxury inversion.",
      "Highly visual and platformable: macro photography, fashion editorials, a limited 'signed repair' mark.",
      "Directly differentiates from resale platforms, which cannot offer the maison's own hand."
    ],
    "main_concerns": [
      "Visible repair may clash with the polish and perfection that clients expect from Dior.",
      "Risk of seeming to romanticize damage or to resemble existing kintsugi-inspired fashion campaigns.",
